<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pánuco River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pánuco River is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> that drains part of the central highlands and reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a>. Its lower valley was the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huastec-people">Huastec people</a> and came under Spanish rule in the early 1520s. In the colonial period it formed the southern edge of the province of Nuevo Santander, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-escandon">José de Escandón</a> in the 1740s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panuco-river.jpg" alt="Pánuco River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horacio_Fernandez · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puente_Tampico_sobre_el_rio_Panuco._-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
